  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Guideline-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder for Bariatric Surgery</Fragment>
      Six low-sugar, high-protein powders for life after a sleeve or bypass, chosen on protein per small serving, lactose and format. Your surgical team has the final word.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#why-protein-matters">Why Protein Matters</a>
      <a href="#how-we-chose">How We Picked</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#protein-targets">Protein Targets</a>
      <a href="#isolate-vs-concentrate">Isolate vs Concentrate</a>
      <a href="#sugar-dumping">Sugar and Dumping</a>
      <a href="#small-volume">Small Volumes</a>
      <a href="#diet-stages">Diet Stages</a>
      <a href="#unflavored-savory">Unflavored and Savory</a>
      <a href="#cost-and-buying">Cost and Testing</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          After bariatric surgery the target is usually at least 60 g of protein a day, from a stomach that may hold only a few ounces at a time. The powders that help most are whey isolates with about 20 g or more of protein per scoop and essentially no sugar. Unjury Unflavored is our default pick because it disappears into food, and the rest of this list covers meal replacements, savory and clear options, and cheaper isolates for later stages. This guide is not medical advice: your surgeon and registered dietitian set your targets and approve your products, and their instructions come first.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-protein-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Protein Becomes the Hard Part After Surgery
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before surgery, protein is something most people never think about. After a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass it becomes the number you track every day, because the operation shrinks how much you can eat at once while your body still needs enough protein to heal incisions, hold on to muscle and keep your hair and immune system in working order.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem is arithmetic. In the early weeks many patients can manage only a few tablespoons of food or a few ounces of liquid at a time, and even months later portions stay small. Chicken breast and Greek yogurt are excellent protein sources, but if you can only eat a quarter cup, you cannot reach a daily target from food alone. That is the gap protein powder fills.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rapid weight loss also puts lean tissue at risk. When calories are very low and protein is short, the body breaks down muscle for the amino acids it needs. Losing some lean mass alongside fat is expected, but most bariatric dietitians push hard on protein precisely because inadequate intake is one of the more common nutrition problems after surgery, and it is largely preventable.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes protein powder a substitute for your program's plan. Surgeons and dietitians set stage-by-stage diets for good reasons, and they differ between hospitals. Treat this guide as a way to understand the choices on the shelf and the questions worth asking, not as instructions. If your team has named a product or a target, follow that over anything you read here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked These Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run a clinical trial or taste these powders in a lab. This guide is built on label analysis, brand and retailer listings checked at the time of writing, and the published bariatric nutrition guidance. Taste and texture comments are reported from buyer feedback, not our own testing, and every nutrition figure should be read as per the label at the time of writing; it can vary by flavor and change with reformulation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The filters were the ones bariatric dietitians tend to use. First, protein density: at least about 20 g of protein in a single serving, so a small drink does real work. Second, sugar: essentially none, ideally under 1 or 2 g, because concentrated sugar is the main trigger for dumping syndrome. Third, protein type: whey isolate or hydrolyzed whey first, for lower lactose and easier digestion.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also weighed format. Unflavored and savory powders scored well because they let you add protein to food rather than relying entirely on shakes, which most people tire of. Serving size mattered too, since a powder that needs 16 fl oz of liquid is hard to finish with a small pouch. Price per serving came last, as a tiebreaker rather than a reason to choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out collagen-only products as primary picks. Collagen is popular in bariatric circles because it dissolves clear, but it lacks tryptophan and is low in several other essential amino acids, so it is not a complete protein. Some programs allow it as an extra; few would count it gram for gram against a whey isolate. Ask before you rely on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our Top Picks for Bariatric Patients
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pick lists about 20 g or more of protein per serving and about 1 g of sugar or less, per the label at the time of writing. Figures vary by flavor; check yours, and check each product with your bariatric team before you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="protein-targets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Protein After Bariatric Surgery
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most authoritative figure comes from the 2019 clinical practice guidelines published jointly by the American Association of Clinical Endocrinologists, The Obesity Society and the American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery, among others. They say protein intake should be individualized and guided by a registered dietitian, with a minimum of 60 g a day and up to 1.5 g per kilogram of ideal body weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same guidance notes that higher intakes, up to about 2.1 g per kilogram of ideal body weight, may be needed in some cases and should be assessed individually. Procedures that bypass more of the intestine, such as the duodenal switch, generally call for higher targets because more protein escapes absorption. In practice, many programs hand patients a figure of 60 to 80 g a day.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Guideline Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">60 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily minimum (2019 guideline)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.5 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ideal body weight, upper usual range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2.1 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Higher intake, individually assessed</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20-30 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per serving in our picks</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Note that the guideline uses ideal body weight, not your current weight. For someone whose ideal body weight is 70 kg, 1.5 g per kilogram works out to about 105 g a day, which is more than most people reach in the first months. That is normal; programs usually build up toward the target as your pouch tolerates more food, and your dietitian will tell you what is realistic at each stage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Research consistently finds that many patients fall short, especially in the first year. That is the practical case for a supplement: two servings of a 21 g powder supply more than 40 g, leaving a much smaller gap for food to close. Our general guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> covers the wider evidence, but after surgery your team's number is the one that counts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="isolate-vs-concentrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Isolate, Concentrate or Hydrolyzed Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bariatric programs start patients on whey protein isolate, and the reason is lactose. Whey concentrate keeps more of the milk sugar and fat, while isolate is filtered further to remove most of both. Some people notice lactose intolerance for the first time after surgery, particularly after gastric bypass, so a powder that is low in lactose avoids one more source of bloating, cramping and diarrhea.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolate also tends to run higher in protein per gram and lower in calories, which suits small servings. Four of our six picks are pure isolate or hydrolyzed whey. The Premier powder is a blend of isolate and concentrate; it is not high in lactose by dairy standards, but it is a later-stage choice for people who already know dairy agrees with them. Our explainer on <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> covers the processing in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrolyzed whey has been partly broken down into shorter peptides. It is the base of most clear proteins, including the Myprotein pick, and some people find it gentler, though it often tastes slightly bitter, which is why clear proteins lean on fruity, acidic flavors. There is no strong evidence that hydrolyzed whey is better absorbed after surgery in a way that changes outcomes.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you cannot have dairy at all, whey is out regardless of type. Pea, soy and egg-white powders can work, though plant proteins often need larger servings for the same protein and can be thicker. Our guide to the <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">best lactose-free protein powder</a> covers dairy-free and lactose-free options, and your dietitian can confirm which suit your stage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sugar-dumping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sugar, Sweeteners and Dumping Syndrome
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dumping syndrome happens when food, especially concentrated sugar, moves too quickly from the stomach into the small intestine. Early dumping typically starts within about 30 minutes of eating and can bring nausea, cramping, a racing heart, sweating and diarrhea. It is most associated with gastric bypass but can occur after a sleeve too, and sugary drinks are among the most common triggers.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why sugar is the first line most dietitians tell patients to read. Many program handouts give a rule of thumb, often a few grams of sugar per serving at most, but the cut-off varies by program, so ask for yours. Every pick on this list sits at or under about 1 g of sugar per serving per the label, and the unflavored Unjury and Isopure contain none.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read sugar, then sweeteners, then serving size.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Low sugar is non-negotiable for most programs. Next, check for sugar alcohols such as sorbitol or maltitol, which can cause gas and diarrhea. Finally, check how much liquid a serving needs; a great label is no help if you cannot finish the drink.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sweeteners are a separate question. Most flavored powders use sucralose, acesulfame potassium, stevia or monk fruit to keep sugar near zero. These do not trigger dumping the way sugar does, but some people find intense sweetness hard to tolerate after surgery, and sugar alcohols can cause gas and loose stools. If flavored shakes start to taste sickly, an unflavored powder in a savory food is the usual workaround.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for meal-replacement shakes and mass gainers that hide sugar behind a protein claim. A shake built for athletes may carry 10 to 20 g of sugar or more alongside its protein, which is exactly what you want to avoid. Unflavored isolate in milk your team has approved, or a purpose-built low-sugar formula, is a safer route than a sports shake chosen on protein alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-volume" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fitting 20 Grams Into a Small Pouch
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein per sip matters more after surgery than almost anywhere else. A powder that delivers 21 g in a 24 g scoop, like Unjury Unflavored, is close to pure protein, so it adds very little bulk to whatever you mix it into. Compare that with a 41 g two-scoop serving that needs a full shaker of liquid, and the difference shows up as a drink you can finish versus one you cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many programs ask patients to sip slowly through the day rather than drink a full shake at once, and to keep liquids separate from solid meals by around 30 minutes. A small shake made with 4 to 6 fl oz of liquid, sipped over 20 to 30 minutes, is easier on a healing pouch than a large one. Your program's own timing rules take priority over any general advice here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature and texture help. Cold, thin drinks tend to go down more easily than thick, room-temperature ones, which is part of why isolates and clear proteins are popular. Blending with ice makes a shake bigger, not easier, so skip it if volume is the limiting factor. For warm savory drinks, keep the liquid below about 140 degrees Fahrenheit so whey does not clump.
          </p>

          <p>
            Splitting servings is fine. Half a scoop in morning yogurt, half in a lunchtime soup and a small shake in the afternoon adds up the same as one large drink, and many people find it more manageable. The overlap with GLP-1 users is real here; our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">best protein powder for GLP-1 users</a> deals with the same small-appetite problem from a different angle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diet-stages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Matching the Powder to Your Diet Stage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most programs move patients through a sequence: clear liquids, full liquids, pureed foods, soft foods and finally a regular texture, over several weeks. The timing and the rules for each stage differ between hospitals, and some skip or merge stages. What follows is a general pattern for thinking about which powder fits where, not a schedule to follow.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What often fits</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ask your team</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear liquids</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clear protein drinks, if allowed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Does clear whey count on this stage?</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full liquids</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Isolate shakes, savory broth-style protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Which milks or bases are allowed?</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pureed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unflavored isolate stirred into purees</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">How much protein per meal is the goal?</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soft and regular</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Food first, powder to close the gap</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">When can I reduce supplements?</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            On clear liquids, some programs allow clear protein drinks and others do not; this is the stage where checking matters most. On full liquids, isolate shakes and savory options such as Unjury Chicken Soup usually become the backbone of the day, because food protein is not yet on the menu. This is when daily protein from supplements is at its highest.
          </p>

          <p>
            On pureed and soft stages, unflavored powder earns its place. Stirred into pureed soups, cottage cheese, yogurt, mashed vegetables or hot cereal, a scoop adds 20 or so grams without changing the texture much. Once regular foods are back, most dietitians shift the emphasis to food protein first, with powder used to close whatever gap remains rather than to replace meals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meal-replacement formulas such as Bariatric Advantage HPMR fit when your team wants a shake to stand in for a small meal. Because they add vitamins and minerals, check them against the bariatric multivitamin, calcium and iron you have been prescribed, so you are not doubling up on some nutrients. Our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">best meal replacement shake</a> covers the wider category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="unflavored-savory" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Unflavored and Savory: Getting Protein Into Food
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shake fatigue is one of the most common complaints in bariatric support groups. Drinking two or three sweet shakes a day for weeks wears down even people who liked the flavor at first, and some develop an outright aversion. Unflavored powder is the main defense, because it moves protein out of the shaker and into food you already eat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Good carriers are moist, mild and not too hot: plain Greek yogurt, cottage cheese, pureed soups, mashed cauliflower, sugar-free pudding, hot cereal once it has cooled slightly, and approved milks. Stir the powder into a small amount of liquid first to make a paste, then fold it in, which avoids dry clumps. Buyers of both Unjury and Isopure unflavored report that a faint dairy taste is the main giveaway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat is the main limit. Whey proteins start to clump and turn grainy in very hot liquids, which is why Unjury tells users to keep its powders below about 140 degrees Fahrenheit. Add powder to soups and cereals after they have cooled from the stove rather than while they are boiling. Baking with protein works too, though most baked goods arrive later in the diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Savory protein drinks fill a different niche. A warm mug of Unjury Chicken Soup or Bariatric Advantage's Chicken Soup flavor feels more like a meal than a dessert, and helps on days when sweetness is the last thing you want. The trade-off is sodium; Unjury's chicken soup lists about 770 mg per scoop. Our guide to the <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> goes further into mixing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-and-buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Cost, Testing and When to Switch Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clinical brands cost more. At the brand's listed price at the time of writing, Unjury runs about $2 per 21 g serving, and Bariatric Advantage HPMR works out to roughly $2.60 per serving. Mainstream isolates such as Isopure Unflavored and grocery powders such as Premier usually come in well under that per gram of protein, especially in larger tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The premium buys format and focus rather than better protein. Bariatric brands offer small tubs, sample packs, savory flavors and products designed around program rules, which is worth paying for in the first weeks when you do not yet know what you tolerate. Our <a href="/unjury-protein-powder-review/">Unjury protein powder review</a> compares that range flavor by flavor, including cost per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many patients start on a clinical brand and switch to a mainstream isolate a few months out, once they know whey isolate agrees with them. That is reasonable if the label meets the same tests: high protein per scoop, essentially no sugar, isolate-based, and no sugar alcohols. Tell your dietitian when you switch so they can check the new label against your plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party testing is worth looking for, but none of our picks carries an NSF Certified for Sport listing that we could confirm, which is common in this category. Buy from reputable sellers, avoid powders making medical or weight-loss claims, and read our guide on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> for what testing covers. If you have kidney disease, take medication or are pregnant, talk to your care team before adding any supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a shake on protein alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sports shake or mass gainer can carry 20 g of protein alongside 10 g or more of sugar, which is a common trigger for dumping syndrome. Read the sugar line before the protein line, and check for sugar alcohols such as sorbitol and maltitol, which can cause gas and diarrhea in a post-surgery gut.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a giant tub in week one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tastes and tolerances change sharply after surgery, and many patients find a flavor they liked beforehand suddenly unbearable. Start with sample packs or small tubs from a bariatric brand, find what you tolerate, then move to a larger, cheaper container. A five-pound tub you cannot face is the most expensive protein there is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring lactose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some people notice lactose intolerance for the first time after surgery, especially after gastric bypass. Whey concentrate and milk-based shakes carry more lactose than isolate. If bloating, cramps or loose stools follow your shakes, tell your dietitian and try a pure isolate or a lactose-free option before deciding protein powder is the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing powder into boiling liquids</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey clumps and turns grainy in very hot liquid, and Unjury tells users to keep its powders below about 140 degrees Fahrenheit. Let soups, broth and hot cereal cool slightly before stirring powder in, and make a paste with a little liquid first. The protein is not ruined, but the texture can make it hard to finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling up on vitamins</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Meal-replacement formulas often add 20 or more vitamins and minerals, while most bariatric patients already take a prescribed multivitamin, calcium and sometimes iron. Stacking them can push some nutrients higher than intended. Show the full label to your dietitian before using a fortified shake every day, and let them adjust your supplement plan.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder after bariatric surgery?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most patients, a whey protein isolate with around 20 g of protein per scoop and essentially no sugar is the right starting point. Unjury Unflavored fits that description and mixes into food, which helps with shake fatigue. The best product for you is the one your bariatric team approves for your stage, so ask them before you buy anything.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein do I need after gastric sleeve or bypass?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The 2019 AACE, TOS and ASMBS guidelines set a minimum of 60 g a day and up to 1.5 g per kilogram of ideal body weight, individualized by a registered dietitian. Higher intakes may be needed after procedures such as the duodenal switch. Many programs give patients a target of 60 to 80 g a day; your dietitian sets yours.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey isolate better than concentrate after bariatric surgery?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually, yes, for early stages. Isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat, so it is gentler for people who develop lactose intolerance after surgery and delivers more protein per gram. Concentrate can be fine later for people who tolerate dairy well. Your program may specify isolate for the first weeks, so check.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein shakes cause dumping syndrome?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Shakes high in sugar can, because concentrated sugar moving quickly into the small intestine is a classic trigger. Low-sugar isolate shakes are much less likely to cause it. Drinking a large volume quickly can also cause discomfort. Choose products with little or no sugar, sip slowly, and report recurring symptoms to your surgical team rather than self-treating.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>When can I start protein shakes after surgery?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  That depends entirely on your program. Many start protein drinks during the liquid stages in the first days or weeks after surgery, but the timing, allowed products and amounts differ between hospitals and procedures. Follow the written diet plan your surgical team gave you, and call them if it does not say whether a product is allowed.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Unjury better than regular protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unjury is not a different kind of protein; it is whey isolate packaged for bariatric patients, with small tubs, savory flavors and no sugar in its unflavored version. That format helps in the first months. Later, a mainstream unflavored isolate with a similar label can do the same job for less money, once your dietitian agrees it fits your plan.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What about clinical brands like Fresubin?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Fresubin is a medical nutrition range from Fresenius Kabi, sold mainly outside the United States and often recommended or prescribed through hospitals. Products in clinical ranges are formulated for specific medical needs and differ widely, so we have not ranked them here. If your care team recommends one, use it as directed; they chose it for your situation.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

